A||||23||Richard Parnell||V8 install chassis mod.||||1||
Z||000000||Richard Parnell||10-23-2000||04:54 PM||||Long post sorry. Just about done with the 350 install into my 87 SC.  There have been many mods we have had to make that are not covered very well in the instructions from v8 archie.  The latest is that we are having to cut the frame where the pulleys stick through.  Not just notch or bend but actually cut and reinforce by boxing to keep the strength needed to handle the V8.  I think the archie kit is made more for the early Fieros especially on this part of the install.  The frame on the 87 seems different than the early models therefore we are having to do this.  Hopefully this week will see the completion of this project.  It has taken a lot more work than I have ever envisioned but I can still hardly wait for the end result.  Anyone thinking of doing this be sure you have another car to use for a while,  have a good experienced mechanic to help and have a well supplied shop with a lift and welding equipment as you will be making and or modifiying brackets for oil filter and alternator (among other things) and modifying the exhaust system that comes with the kit, it's not cut to fit. You have to do the actual fitting of the pipes. The mufflers that were sent (even though I asked for cats) are just glass packs, so I will be buying some small flowmasters to replace those.  There's plenty of room to do this the way we have routed the exhaust.For the water hose we have made a pipe to go over the top of the engine and fitted it back against the firewall instead of using the supplied water hoses, which would have needed to be spliced together to make the length needed. There's no need to buy the most expensive kit as a lot of the hoses and fittings are not adequate for the project and you will be going out and getting items that are of better quality than is supplied. ie. brass fittings instead of cast plumbing type fittings, etc. Not meant as a slam here, but there is a lot more work to this project than you would think.||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfixit58||10-24-2000||08:03 AM||||Richard,<P>Thanks for posting your progress. I would be interested in seeing some pictures of the details you've mentioned. How much motor are you using?<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000002||Richard Parnell||10-24-2000||01:35 PM||||The motor is a rebuilt stock 350 from an old Caprice. It has maybe 12k miles on it and has a cam for towing a trailer.  It's probably too late for detailed pictures on some of the brackets we made but I'll post what I can. I'll make some drawings of what I can't get the camera on if people are interested.  We are going with the stock Chevy fuel pump and getting rid of the in tank fuel pump. There will be pictures of the finished product.  It's not a pretty motor with a lot of chrome tho I do plan on some nice valve covers eventually.  I'm more interested in the go than the show right now.  Once I get the body and suspension to where I want it I will get another cradle and start building up a crate motor.  But that's just a pipe dream right now.  I can hardly wait to get the thing on the road and learn how to drive it without killing myself.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Richard Parnell||10-24-2000||02:34 PM||||The motor is a rebuilt stock 350 from an old Caprice.  The only thing done to it is a cam for towing trailers, so it has extra low end.  It may be a little late for pictures of some of the bracket modifications but I will post pics of what I can get the camera on.  If anyone's interested I can make some drawings of the bracket changes and oil fitting routings, etc and post them. We did go with the stock Chevy fuel pump and dumped the in tank Fiero fuel pump.  It's a personal choice between me and my mechanic buddy.  He has had problems with the electric pumps and prefers the Chevy one. I will take pics of the finished product.  It isn't a pretty engine (orange block and valve covers) as I'm more interested in go than show right now.  I do plan on putting some nice valve covers on and probably a few shiney dodahs here and there.  Once the body and suspension are up to snuff I plan on getting another cradle and building up a crate engine with extra poop.  As it is each week I say this is the week and THIS week will be the week that it's done.  I can hardly wait.  I just need to get it on the road and learn out to drive it without killing myself.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Richard Parnell||10-24-2000||02:36 PM||||Sorry for the double post.  I posted an hour ago and didn't see the first reply so redid it and voila they both popped up.  Cliff?||||reg||1
Z||000005||mrfixit58||10-24-2000||02:38 PM||||Sounds great Richard. I've driven in Hawaii and know there are a LOT of twisty-turny roads where a Fiero would be great fun. <P>I've toyed with the idea of building a V8 Fiero ever since I bought my first one. One of the ideas I've had was to make my own brackets and use rubber motor mounts with solid cradle bushings (i.g., 1988). I think it would help keep the suspension geometry from changing under hard acceleration. <P>I think with if I could come up with the motor/tranny adaptor, I could fabricate the rest. I've got a little experience in motor conversions. Back in the late 1970's I installed a SBC and TH350 into a Chevy LUV truck. It wasn't cheap then and I'm sure it isn't any cheaper now but, it sure sounds like a fun project.<P>Keep us posted,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000006||HarryG||10-24-2000||03:12 PM||||Richard,<BR>You're using Archie's harmonic balancer/pulley and you still had to cut the frame?  I have Archie's "install" video (383 into an '88 chassis) and besides the hole for the water pump, the only cutting shown is 2 cuts and folding over of the upper frame lip (for water pump clearance).<BR>Did you ask Archie about your lack of space?<BR>Harry   ||||reg||5
Z||000007||Richard Parnell||10-24-2000||04:48 PM||||I'm having a hard time getting replies from Archie.  Since I want to get things done and I don't have days to wait between replies we're charging ahead with what we can dtermine to be the best moves.||||reg||1
Z||000008||HarryG||10-25-2000||11:49 AM||||Richard,<BR>I think Archie should be back from vacation now.<BR>Unless he's chasing parts, you can usually reach him at the shop on the 800 number. Or you could try Technet; he monitors that pretty closely.<BR>Harry||||reg||1
Z||000009||Zorba||10-25-2000||12:24 PM||||I suspect that if the elongated (2) holes were too long, that the  h.b. pulley might touch the frame rail. I had to do some frame cutting to get mine to fit and am re-enforcing where I did some trimming. However, changing the belt on mine should be a breeze (clearance now). For the remote oil filter, you only need a bracket if you mount the filter any other way than what archie suggests. I think his is mounted on the cradle...drilling a hole on the fiero cradle is a pain in the butt. The cradle appears to be stronger than the frame itself!<BR>Areas where I think more clarification would be adequate with a v8 conversion are:<P>1. Wiring <BR>2. If you use a CSI electric water pump, you will need to bend the frame rail lips and cut a hole(s) on the wheel well for r & r and for pump motor clearance. In addition, if you use a CSI electric water pump, you will need to find a fitting (moroso has one) to hook up one of the heater hoses).<BR>3. More plumbing details. The idea of using a pipe from left to right instead of a big long hose is better. A 1.25 1.50 " muffler pipe should do the job. $5.<P>I think the v8 conversion is a great thing for the fiero. The car should have come from the factory with this kind of power. I hated my wimpy 4 or 6 cylinder output. <P>I still think the v8 archie kit is the best.<P>I am considering a 4 core radiator along with an oil cooler for my conversion.||||reg||1
Z||000010||elfiero||10-25-2000||12:50 PM||||Mrfixit58,email me and i'll tell you how to<BR>do it using solid cradle bushings and rubber <BR>mounts for the engine!this includes serpentine drive belt w/A/C.||||reg||6
Z||000011||Richard Parnell||10-25-2000||01:48 PM||||I agree the Archie kit is the best but there are always mods you have to do to get things to fit.  The cutting we had to do was to allow the bolts holding the harmonic balancer to clear the frame.  We notched about 1/2 inch out of the frame and reinforced it to allow the bolts to clear and allow for changing of the belts when needed.  The water pump we mounted upside down. The oil filter we mounted back by the clutch slave cylinder which makes changing very easy.  Finding an AC compressor with the v belt pulley on the clutch took some searching but we have it now and adjusting the fit on the bracket below the alternator is going on right now.  Then we only have the wiring to feed back into the passenger compartment and reconnect and the radiator to change out. Oh yes we decided to use the 4 cyl Fiero fuel pump for now as there is plenty of room to reinstall the mechanical Chevy pump if we have problems.  We weren't aware that there was such a difference in the pressure between the 6 cyl and 4 cyl Fiero in tank pumps so we want to give the 4 cyl pump a try. One more item, even though we have low profile valve covers the notch still has to be cut out of the right hand deck hinge.||||reg||1
Z||000012||mrfixit58||10-25-2000||02:38 PM||||elfiero,<P>You've got mail!<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000013||Zorba||10-25-2000||05:06 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Richard Parnell:<BR><B> The cutting we had to do was to allow the bolts holding the harmonic balancer to clear the frame.  We notched about 1/2 inch out of the frame and reinforced it to allow the bolts to clear and allow for changing of the belts when needed .</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Note that I am in the process of building a v8 right alongside with you... But you mention bolts...there are NO bolts on the harmonic balancer, center or otherwise. It is pressed in with a h.b. installer tool.<BR>Am I missing something here? I had to notch the frame because I believe I elongated the cradle slots a bit too much which created a slight offset of distance between the h.b. pulley and the frame rail. One thing. That pulley has to be installed as close to the timing chain cover as possible...something like .0300" without touching it. If you have the video, it tells you exactly how it has to be installed. I am curious to know how you have set up other things, if carburated, wiring and the plumbing. I elected to used a CSI electric water pump on mine.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Richard Parnell||10-25-2000||06:20 PM||||The pulley on the bottom of the engine is what I'm talking about.  The one that drives the water pump.  On the kit I was sent it has bolts where it connects to the engine.  The thing that got me is that the pulley itself that the belt goes over is much smaller than the one pictured in the instructions.  I wonder if I was sent the right one?  My mechanic buddy doesn't seem worried about it though.  We tack welded the pulley onto the shaft to position it out to line up with the water pump. The engine is carburated.  There is no computer on board doing anything anymore, which is how I like it.  The car is just spark, gas and brakes just like the used to be.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Richard Parnell||10-26-2000||04:17 PM||||Further update.  Routing the fuel lines today.  The car's way too laoud, I'll wake the neighbors everytime I start it in the morning.  So some flowmasters look in order.  Anyone know of a good source for those?||||reg||1
Z||000016||Zorba||10-27-2000||02:12 AM|||| That's why I used a CSI water electric water pump. But the mechanical one is more proven and possibly more reliable in the long run. I am curious as to how you wired your car off the wiring harness and how you did your fuel lines. That's exactly where I am at right now. I can figure this out but could use your set up to save time on mine.<BR>All I have left: wiring, carburator, spark plug wires, fuel lines and plumbing.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Richard Parnell||10-27-2000||02:12 PM||||Zorba-  As soon as I have some time I'll draw up the gas line and water line routings and whatever electrical I can and post .  The spark plug wires, we're routing the fire wall side wires away from the exhaust with a harness attached to the firewall.  We're using the longest wire kit we could get so we could custom cut the lengths.  You can use the Impala wires if a kit can't be found also the large block wires are good.  I've decided to use a new Edelbrock 600 carb with an adapter plate for the quadra jet.  We'll not be using a choke.  As I said earlier we made a long pipe for the water to route over the engine mounted on the firewall instead of rubber.  The rest of the hoses are routed differently than the instructions to avoid going throught the inner body panels.  The same with the fuel lines.  I'll keep posting as new developements arise.  We are planning on firing it up tomorrow.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Eric Marsh||10-27-2000||04:26 PM||||I know what you mean. I had to do the same cutting and welding on my '88. I also had to seriously notch the K-member to get the A/C compressort to fit. The engine fits, but everything is "just barely"<P>Eric||||reg||1
Z||000019||Richard Parnell||10-29-2000||12:36 AM||||Started the engine up today.  It wasn't near a loud as I thought.  It sounded throaty and mean with an understatement of power .  The car won't be drivable until weds or thurs next week but I plan on takin a cruise around the island next sat.  I just can't get over how cool it sounded.  We had to start it up several times for people that came by the shop.  Can you say wet dream??? <BR>I did break down and get new edelbrock valve covers to show it up a bit.  We're setting the fuel pressure at 6 lbs. for the edelbrock 600 carb. Yeahh!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000020||batboy||10-29-2000||10:03 AM||||Hope you didn't get the tall valve covers. If you did, have fun getting the firewall side to fit under the deck hinge. At least there is no way I can on my V8 Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000021||Richard Parnell||10-29-2000||03:39 PM||||You're right on that.  I got the low profile covers, but we still had to cut the hinge back a bit.||||reg||1
Z||000022||Zorba||10-30-2000||10:42 AM||||What have you used for a throttle cable?<BR>I found my original to be too long. I bought a throttle cable bracket from Checker Auto and it still wouldn't work right. I ended up using a new cable. A little tricky but I think I got it figured out. It's the little thing that bite ya!||||reg||1
Z||000023||Zorba||10-30-2000||10:49 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR> You can use the Impala wires if a kit can't be found also the large block wires are good.[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What year impala? Does it matter. I have those RAM HORN manifolds instead of headers and I think that "straight plugs" are best as the 90d ones tend to be too close to the manifold. I have routed the water lines and the fuel lines, fuel filter, regulator, and gauge at the carb (600 cfm, edelbrock, elec.)<BR>I am plugging off the return fuel line. I routed  everything away from the exhaust manifolds. I just need to figure out which spark plugs wires are best and get started on the "WIRING" the engine part. plus the exhaust system, of course. It never ends.<P>||||reg||1
